  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Frost/freeze conditions tonight through Sunday \nnight.\n\nThe boundary layer is quickly decoupling this evening with calm\nto light/variable winds already being reported at a number of\nlocations. Clear skies and calm/light winds will persist for \nmuch of the night, helping to maximize radiational cooling. The \nleading edge of a band of thicker cirrus will approach from the \nsouthwest Sunday morning, but most of this will not move into \nthe area until after daybreak having little/no impact on\novernight lows. 14/23z temperatures were already running a few \ndegree cooler than expected and given how temperatures \ntranspired this morning, a slight lowering of lows for Sunday \nmorning was needed in places, especially in the coastal \ncorridor. \n\nThe updated forecast expanded the freezing line east and south \na bit which necessitated an upgrade of the Frost Advisory to a \nFreeze Warning for parts of Long, Inland Liberty and Inland \nBryan Counties. Most of the RAWS sites in the open areas of Fort\nStewart dropped to 33-34 this morning, so reaching freezing \nlooks quite plausible in these same areas for Sunday morning. \nThe Frost Advisory was also expanded into Coastal Jasper County \ngiven the slightly colder temperatures and greater risk for \nfrost there. Freezing temperatures may briefly reach into the \nfar northern portions of Tidal Berkeley County and possibly the \nfar interior areas of upper Charleston County just before \ndaybreak, but the impacted area look too small to justify an \nupgrade to a Freeze Warning at this time.\n\nIf widespread freezing temperatures occur tonight into Sunday \nmorning, then this will end the local growing season and \nFrost/Freeze products will not be issued for Sunday night into \nMonday morning. However, if only some areas observe freezing \ntemperatures, then the growing season continues and we will \nlikely need Frost/Freeze products on Sunday night into Monday \nmorning. Regardless of the outcome, overnight temperatures will \nbe below normal for this time of year.",
